 The problem involves finding the two possible values of \(\angle B\) in a triangle given \(AB = 13\), \(BC = 10\), and \(\angle A = 40^\circ\). We can use the Law of Sines to solve for \(\angle B\). The Law of Sines states that \(\frac{a}{\sin(A)} = \frac{b}{\sin(B)} = \frac{c}{\sin(C)}\). Here, \(a = BC = 10\), \(b = AC\), \(c = AB = 13\), and \(\angle A = 40^\circ\). We can use the Law of Sines to express \(\sin(B)\) in terms of the known values. \[
\frac{10}{\sin(B)} = \frac{13}{\sin(40^\circ)}
\] Solving for \(\sin(B)\), we get \[
\sin(B) = \frac{10 \cdot \sin(40^\circ)}{13}
\] We then need to find two possible values for \(\angle B\). Since \(\sin(B) = \frac{10 \cdot \sin(40^\circ)}{13}\), the two possible values for \(B\) are \(\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{10 \cdot \sin(40^\circ)}{13}\right)\) and \(180^\circ - \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{10 \cdot \sin(40^\circ)}{13}\right)\). The sum of the two possible values of \(\angle B\) is \(180^\circ\). </think> </think> <answer>180</answer>

